>> title: Saba Shioyaki
>> category: Smoked & Grilled
>> tags: japanese, mackerel, fish, grilled, broiler, weeknight
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: さばの塩焼き, saba shioyaki, saba shioyaki, 鯖の塩焼き, サバの塩焼き, 焼き魚, yakizakana, salt-grilled mackerel, grilled mackerel
>> pairs-with: gohan, tonjiru
>> servings: 2
>> time: 45 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the gap between done and dry under a broiler is about a minute wide, and nothing puts the rendered fat back
>> step.1: Under a broiler the heat is further away, so the skin takes longer to blister and the flesh longer to dry.
>> step.2: salt, rest 30 min
>> step.3: brush with sake
>> step.4: broil 8 min, skin up
>> step.5: top with grated daikon

In Japan this is cooked in a 魚焼きグリル, a shallow drawer under the burner built for nothing else.

Salt @mackerel fillets{1%lb}(450 g; 2 fillets, skin scored twice on the diagonal) with @fine sea salt{1 1/2%tsp}(scattered from a foot up so it lands evenly) on a rack and leave them ~cure{30%min}, until beads of water stand on the surface.

Blot @&(~1)salted mackerel{} dry and brush it with @sake{1%Tbs}(15 mL), which takes the last of it and helps the skin colour rather than just dry.

Broil @&(~1)mackerel{} skin-side up about 4 inches under the element ~broil{8%min} without turning, until the skin has blistered and split and the thickest part flakes.

Serve @&(~1)saba shioyaki{} with @grated daikon{1/2%cup}(120 mL; squeezed just once), @soy sauce{1%tsp} poured over the daikon and not the fish, and @lemon{1%wedge}.
